        <div class="prose">
          <h2>A harbour town with tighter streets</h2>
          <p>Stonehaven's older streets, particularly around the harbour, are narrower and more tightly packed than the newer parts of town, and parking can be limited at busy times. If your car breaks down near the harbour or in the older part of town, let us know as much as you can about the street width and any parking restrictions nearby — it helps us bring the right vehicle and plan how to load safely.</p>

          <h2>Two very different roads</h2>
          <p>Most Stonehaven breakdowns we attend are on one of two roads. The A90 passes the town linking it to Aberdeen and Portlethen to the north and continuing south towards Angus, and carries fast-moving traffic much like any other stretch of the A90. The A957, known locally as the Slug Road, heads inland from Stonehaven towards Banchory and the wider Deeside area — it's a hillier, more exposed road than the A90, with tighter bends and, in poor weather, a genuinely different driving experience. If you break down on the Slug Road, tell us that specifically, since it changes how we plan the recovery compared with a straightforward A90 job.</p>

          <h2>Seasonal visitor traffic</h2>
          <p>Stonehaven is popular with visitors, particularly through the summer months, and the town's roads and car parks see noticeably more traffic and tighter parking at these times. A breakdown during a busy summer weekend can mean more congestion to work around, so extra patience and clear directions help us reach you as smoothly as possible.</p>

          <div class="callout">
            <p><strong>Broken down on the Slug Road?</strong> This is a hillier, more rural route with fewer passing places than the A90. Get well off the carriageway if you can, keep warm and visible, and let us know your direction of travel and the nearest landmark or mile marker if you've seen one.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

